## Attack 4 — the tripwire test

- `masterBaseline=19` independently re-derived: ran a throwaway Go snippet
  using the exact counting method the test uses
  (`strings.Split(body, "\n")` + `strings.TrimSpace`) against the master
  scratch's own `/dashboard/kpi/expenses` render → 19. Matches the branch's
  render → 19. Confirmed master's own figure, not an assumed constant.
- Reverted the trim in a scratch copy only (`sed` on the scratch file,
  never the real tree) and re-ran the test: it FAILS (`got=24`,
  `want=19` — the same defect class attempt-3 caught reappears and is
  caught). Not vacuous.
- Limitation (observation, not FAIL, per the calibration rule): this is a
  COUNT-based tripwire, not a content/structural one — a hypothetical
  template edit that removes one whitespace-only line while adding a
  visible-content anomaly elsewhere, netting back to 19, would not be
  caught by this test alone. The count is honestly derived and does catch
  the actual defect class (untrimmed action produces/removes a text node),
  which is what it was built for.

